---
import LessonPage from "../../../components/lesson/LessonPage.astro";
import { defineLesson } from "../../../data/lesson-schema.mjs";
export const page = defineLesson({
  "title": "First conditional · A2 Grammar · Curriculum Map",
  "slug": "first-conditional",
  "level": "A2",
  "topic": "First conditional",
  "category": "Grammar",
  "contentType": "grammar",
  "status": "ready",
  "description": "Connect a possible future with its likely result: If it rains, we’ll stay home , If I finish early, I’ll call you , and What will you do if the flight is late?",
  "sequence": 9,
  "prerequisites": [],
  "related": [],
  "assessments": [
    "a2-exit"
  ]
});
---
<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 9</p>
  <h1>First conditional <em>if + will</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Connect a possible future with its likely result: <span class="ex">If it rains, we’ll stay home</span>, <span class="ex">If I finish early, I’ll call you</span>, and <span class="ex">What will you do if the flight is late?</span></p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">≈ 55–65 min</span>
    <span class="tag">if + present simple</span>
    <span class="tag">will + base verb</span>
    <span class="tag">when or if</span>
    <span class="tag">11 interactive tasks</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Find the two halves of one future sentence.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>If + present simple, will + base verb.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Choose forms, join clauses, and repair errors.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Make real plans that depend on conditions.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">The first conditional joins two future ideas: a condition that might happen and the result you expect. <span class="ex">If</span> is the hinge between them.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill>
    <h3>Three quick checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Answer from memory, then check. Say each complete sentence aloud.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will">will</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="won’t">won’t</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="starts">starts</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="start">start</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Open">Open</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Opens">Opens</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>I think it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> rain tomorrow. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(will for predictions · A2 Lesson 4)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The meeting <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="starts" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> at nine every day. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(present simple · A1)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Open" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the window, please. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(imperatives · A1)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Three messages about tomorrow</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">"If I finish early, I’ll call you."</span><br /><span class="ex">"If the traffic is bad, I’ll take the train."</span><br /><span class="ex">"I won’t wait if the bus is late."</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Each sentence has two halves. Which half starts with <span class="ex">if</span>?</li>
      <li>Look at the verbs after <span class="ex">if</span>: <span class="ex">finish</span>, <span class="ex">is</span>. Are they future forms?</li>
      <li>Where does <span class="ex">’ll</span> or <span class="ex">won’t</span> live? Which half of each sentence?</li>
      <li>The last sentence puts the <span class="ex">if</span> half second. What happened to the comma?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill>
    <h3>Discovery check: choose the pattern</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Find the <b>if</b> half first. It takes the present simple. The other half takes <b>will</b>.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="rains">rains</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will rain">will rain</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is">is</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will be">will be</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="’ll stay">’ll stay</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="finishes">finishes</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>If it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="rains" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> on Sunday, we’ll stay home.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>If it rains, we <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="’ll stay" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> home.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>If Marco <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="finishes" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> early, he’ll join us.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>We’ll eat outside if the weather <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> good.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>If the traffic <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> bad, I’ll take the train.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The roads <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will be" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> wet if it rains tonight.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The system: one condition, one result</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A first conditional sentence has two clause boxes. The <strong>condition clause</strong> starts with <span class="ex">if</span> and uses the present simple. The <strong>result clause</strong> uses <span class="ex">will</span> or <span class="ex">won’t</span> plus the base verb.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>clause</th><th>verb form</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>condition clause</b></td><td><b>if + present simple</b></td><td><span class="contraction">if it rains · if I finish early</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>result clause</b></td><td><b>will / won’t + base verb</b></td><td><span class="contraction">we’ll stay home · I won’t wait</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>The two clauses can come in either order. Only the comma changes.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>order</th><th>punctuation</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>if-clause first</b></td><td>comma after the condition</td><td><span class="contraction">If it rains, we’ll stay home.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>result first</b></td><td>no comma</td><td><span class="contraction">We’ll stay home if it rains.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Both orders mean exactly the same thing. Choose the order that puts the more important idea first.</p>

  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>The trap: no will inside the if-clause</h3>
    <p>Say <span class="ex">If it rains, we’ll stay home.</span> Do not say <span class="ex">If it will rain, we’ll stay home.</span></p>
    <p>The whole sentence is about the future, but the if-clause borrows the present simple. One <span class="ex">will</span> per sentence, and it always sits in the result clause. If you hear yourself say <span class="ex">if it will</span> or <span class="ex">if I will</span>, stop and repair it.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: a real possible future</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The first conditional describes a real possibility, not imagination. The if-clause names a situation that might truly happen. The result clause predicts what will follow. In <span class="ex">If I finish early, I’ll call you</span>, the present simple <span class="ex">finish</span> does not mean present time. It simply marks the condition, and the future meaning comes from the sentence as a whole.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>part</th><th>job</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>if-clause</b></td><td>names a possible situation</td><td><span class="contraction">If I finish early… (maybe I will, maybe I won’t)</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>result clause</b></td><td>predicts the likely result</td><td><span class="contraction">…I’ll call you. (my plan if it happens)</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill>
    <h3>Drill 1: choose the correct form</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">First find the if-clause. Present simple after <b>if</b>; <b>will</b> only in the result.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="rains">rains</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will rain">will rain</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="finishes">finishes</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will finish">will finish</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="’ll call">’ll call</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="call">call</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="won’t">won’t</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="don’t">don’t</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>If it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="rains" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> on Friday, we’ll work from home.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>If Sara <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="finishes" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the report today, she’ll send it tonight.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>If I finish early, I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="’ll call" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> you.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>If you <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="call" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> me after six, I’ll answer.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="won’t" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> go out if the weather is bad.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>If they <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="don’t" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> hurry, they’ll miss the train.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>If the movie <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="finishes" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> late, I’ll take a taxi.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>If these clouds get darker, it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will rain" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> soon.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2><em>When</em> or <em>if</em>?</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Use <span class="ex">when</span> for a future you are sure about. Use <span class="ex">if</span> for a future that is only possible.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>word</th><th>meaning</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>when</b></td><td>you are sure it will happen</td><td><span class="contraction">When I get home, I’ll call you. (I get home every evening.)</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>if</b></td><td>it might happen, it might not</td><td><span class="contraction">If I get home early, I’ll cook dinner. (Maybe early, maybe late.)</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Both words take the present simple, not <span class="ex">will</span>: <span class="ex">when I get home</span>, <span class="ex">if I get home early</span>.</p>

  <h3>More than will in the result</h3>
  <p>At this level the result clause usually uses <span class="ex">will</span> or <span class="ex">won’t</span>, but two other results are common and natural.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>result type</th><th>example</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>will / won’t</b></td><td>If you leave now, you won’t miss the train.</td><td>prediction</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>can</b></td><td>If you finish early, you can go home.</td><td>permission or possibility</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>imperative</b></td><td>If you’re tired, take a break.</td><td>advice or instruction</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="practice" data-answer-drill>
    <h3>Drill 2: type the missing form</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Type one or two words. Present simple after <b>if</b> and <b>when</b>; <b>will</b> in the result.</p>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>If it <input class="answer-input" data-answer="rains" aria-label="answer 1" /> on Saturday, we’ll watch a movie at home. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(rain)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>If Ana <input class="answer-input" data-answer="finishes" aria-label="answer 2" /> work early, she’ll join us for dinner. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(finish)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>If I miss the bus, I <input class="answer-input" data-answer="will take" aria-label="answer 3" /> a taxi. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(take)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>We <input class="answer-input" data-answer="won’t" aria-label="answer 4" /> wait if you’re late. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(will not)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>What <input class="answer-input" data-answer="will" aria-label="answer 5" /> you do if the flight is delayed?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span><input class="answer-input" data-answer="When" aria-label="answer 6" /> I get home tonight, I’ll call you. I get home at six every evening. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(when or if)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-answers>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-answers>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Read and choose</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>A group message about Saturday</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">"Hi everyone! Here’s the plan for Saturday. If it rains, we’ll move the barbecue inside, so don’t cancel anything. If Marco finishes work early, he’ll bring the drinks. If he can’t come before six, I’ll pick them up on my way. When the food is ready, I’ll send a message to the group. See you all there!"</span></p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Good reading. You followed a real plan built from conditions.">
    <h3>Drill 3: read, then choose</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="move the barbecue inside">
        <p class="quiz-question">1. What will they do if it rains?</p>
        <div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="move the barbecue inside">move the barbecue inside</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="cancel the barbecue">cancel the barbecue</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="move it to Sunday">move it to Sunday</button></div>
      </div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Marco">
        <p class="quiz-question">2. Who will bring the drinks if Marco finishes work early?</p>
        <div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Marco">Marco</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the writer">the writer</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="nobody">nobody</button></div>
      </div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The writer is sure the food will be ready.">
        <p class="quiz-question">3. The writer says “when the food is ready,” not “if.” Why?</p>
        <div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The writer is sure the food will be ready.">The writer is sure the food will be ready.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The food might not be ready.">The food might not be ready.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The food is already ready now.">The food is already ready now.</button></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="we’ll take a taxi if it rains">
    <h3>Drill 4: build the sentence, result first</h3>
    <p class="tile-target">Start with the result. With this order, no comma is needed.</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="rains">rains</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="taxi">taxi</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="we’ll">We’ll</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="if">if</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="take">take</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="it">it</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="a">a</button>
    </div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="what will you do if the bus is late">
    <h3>Drill 5: build the question</h3>
    <p class="tile-target">Start with the question word. The if-clause keeps the present simple.</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="late">late</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="you">you</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="what">What</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="bus">bus</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="will">will</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="if">if</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="do">do</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="is">is</button>
      <button class="tile" data-build-tile="the">the</button>
    </div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice and repair errors</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="will" data-fix="If it rains, we’ll stay home." data-why="Will never goes inside the if-clause. Use the present simple after if: If it rains.">
    <h3>Drill 6: one will per sentence</h3>
    <p>Tap the word that is wrong.</p>
    <div class="spot-options">
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="if">If</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="it">it</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="will">will</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="rain">rain,</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="we’ll">we’ll</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="stay home">stay home.</button>
    </div>
    <p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="finish" data-fix="If she finishes early, she’ll join us." data-why="The if-clause uses the present simple, and after she the verb needs -es: finishes.">
    <h3>Drill 7: present simple still agrees</h3>
    <p>Tap the word that is wrong.</p>
    <div class="spot-options">
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="if">If</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="she">she</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="finish">finish</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="early">early,</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="she’ll">she’ll</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="join us">join us.</button>
    </div>
    <p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="micro-drill" data-transform>
    <h3>Drill 8: say it, then reveal</h3>
    <p>Read the cue, say the full sentence aloud, then reveal only after you answer.</p>
    <div class="transform-list">
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>1</b> I finish early. I call you. Join the two ideas with if.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>If I finish early, I’ll call you.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>2</b> If I finish early, I’ll call you. Say it with the result first.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>I’ll call you if I finish early.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>3</b> If it rains, we’ll walk to the station. Make the result negative.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>If it rains, we won’t walk to the station.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>4</b> If I miss the bus, I’ll be late. Change to he.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>If he misses the bus, he’ll be late.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>5</b> I get home. Then I cook dinner. You are sure this will happen. Join with when.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>When I get home, I’ll cook dinner.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>6</b> A coworker is tired. Give advice with if and an imperative: tired, take a break.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>If you’re tired, take a break.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Use it in real life</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Weekend plan B:</b> Say three sentences about your weekend with <span class="ex">if</span>. <span class="ex">If it rains, I’ll stay home and cook. If my friend calls, we’ll meet downtown.</span></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Ask the teacher:</b> Ask three <span class="ex">What will you do if…?</span> questions. Listen and give one follow-up question. <span class="ex">What will you do if it rains on Sunday?</span></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>At work:</b> Describe two possible situations at your job and their results. <span class="ex">If a client calls after five, I’ll answer tomorrow. If the meeting finishes early, I’ll go to the gym.</span></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Give advice:</b> Give a friend two pieces of advice with <span class="ex">if</span> plus an imperative. <span class="ex">If you feel sick, stay home. If the store is closed, order online.</span></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Sure or possible:</b> Say one sentence with <span class="ex">when</span> and one with <span class="ex">if</span> about tonight. <span class="ex">When I get home, I’ll have dinner. If I’m not tired, I’ll watch a movie.</span></div>

  <h2>Reflect</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Say the system back</h3>
    <p>Complete this aloud: “After if, I use the ______ simple, never will. The result clause uses ______ plus the base verb. If the if-clause comes first, I write a ______. I use when if I am ______ it will happen, and if when it is only ______.”</p>
    <p>Then say two first conditional sentences about your real week: one with the if-clause first and one with the result first.</p>
  </div>

